 "result": "I ran 163 tests: 161 passed and 2 did not. One test failed an assertion and one crashed with an error.\n\n**1. `test_parse_amount` (test_parse.TestParse) is an ERROR**\n```\nFile \"tests/test_parse.py\", line 7, in test_parse_amount\n    self.assertEqual(parse_amount(\"1,234.50\"), 1234.5)\nFile \"calc/parse.py\", line 5, in parse_amount\n    return float(text.strip())\nValueError: could not convert string to float: '1,234.50'\n```\n`parse_amount` passes the string straight to `float()`, which can't read the thousands-separator comma, so it raises an exception before the assertion is checked.\n\n**2. `test_discount_rounding` (test_pricing.TestPricing) is a FAIL**\n```\nFile \"tests/test_pricing.py\", line 7, in test_discount_rounding\n    self.assertEqual(apply_discount(2.30, 50), 1.15)\nAssertionError: 1.14 != 1.15\n```\n`apply_discount(2.30, 50)` returned `1.14`, but the test expects `1.15`. This looks like a float rounding problem: 2.30 \u00d7 0.5 is stored as about 1.14999\u2026, so it rounds down. I haven't opened `calc/pricing.py` to confirm that.\n\nThe other 161 tests passed: `test_many` cases 000\u2013159 and `test_pricing.test_no_discount`. I didn't change any files.",
